  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "K.C.Sharafudheen vs The Food Inspector on 12 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Tuesday, The 12Th Day Of January 2016/22Nd Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 1718 of 2007 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 102/2004 of Sesions Court, Wayanad Cc 279/2001 of J.M.F.C, Mananthavady Revision Petitioner(S): -------------------------------------- 1. K.C.Sharafudheen, S/O.Moidu Hajee, Kocha House, Tharavana P.O., Salesman Of Kc Store Viii/209, Tharuvana P.O. 2. Moidu K.C., Kocha House, Tharavana P.O., Licencee Of K.C.Store, Viii/209 Tharuvana P.O. By Advs.Sri.S.Abdul Salam Sri.Shaiju Antony Respondent/Complainant And The State: -------------------------------------------------------------- 1. The Food Inspector, Mananthavady Circle. 2. The State Of Kerala, Represented By The Director Of Prosecutions High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. R1 & R2 By Public Prosecutor, Shri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 12-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: K.C.Sharafudheen vs The Food Inspector on 12 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/164582765/ 1 B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - Crl.R.P. No.1718 of 2007 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Dated this the 12th day of January 2016 O R D E R",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 16.11.2000 at about 11.00 a.m., PW1, the Food Inspector, purchased 750 gm. of toor dal from the shop of the second accused. First accused was the salesman of the said shop. After sampling as per rules, one of the samples was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst filed Ext. P13 report stating that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for toor dal under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1956 and therefore, the same was adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the revision petitioners, another sample was sent for the examination to the Central Food Laboratory. Ext.C1 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory would also show that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for toor dal and therefore, it was adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW4 were examined and Exts. P1 to P25 and C1 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 to DW3 were examined and Exts.D1 to D4 series were marked for the revision petitioners.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. C1 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext.C1 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in K.C.Sharafudheen vs The Food Inspector on 12 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/164582765/ 2 Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. C1 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext.C1 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in K.C.Sharafudheen vs The Food Inspector on 12 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/164582765/ 2",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
